= Deductive reasoning — Technical =

== Semantic validity ==
For premises Γ and conclusion φ:

<pre>
Γ ⊨ φ
</pre>

means every model satisfying Γ also satisfies φ.

== Syntactic derivability ==
Under a proof calculus:

<pre>
Γ ⊢ φ
</pre>

means φ can be derived from Γ using the permitted rules.

A sound and complete calculus aligns the two relations.

== Monotonicity ==
Classical deduction is monotonic:

<pre>
Γ ⊢ φ  ⇒  Γ ∪ Δ ⊢ φ
</pre>

Adding premises cannot invalidate a previous derivation.

Defeasible and nonmonotonic reasoning intentionally relax this property.

== Psychological implementation ==
Formal validity does not determine the cognitive mechanism used by a human reasoner.

Mental-model, rule-based, probabilistic, and heuristic accounts can explain performance on deductive tasks.

